  "summary": "Healthcare costs continued their upward trajectory this week, with working-age Americans facing a 9% jump in family health insurance premiums while uninsured rates rose and half of uninsured people skipped needed care due to cost concerns. Major employers including Amazon and Google are backing CMS efforts to streamline prior authorization, signaling industry recognition that administrative burdens need addressing. On the regulatory and compliance front, Gilead agreed to a $45 million settlement for kickback allegations in Texas Medicaid, while the FDA finalized guidance on prescription drug-related software and Treasury appears poised to propose restrictions on U.S. biotech investment in China. Clinical innovation headlines included positive data from Moderna and Merck's personalized mRNA cancer vaccine showing a 42% reduction in melanoma recurrence risk, GSK's accelerated approval for a rectal cancer immunotherapy, and a potentially functional cure for hepatitis B, while Eko Health launched an AI-enabled stethoscope and concerns mount over telehealth companies prescribing GLP-1s off-label for addiction without adequate FDA oversight. AI continues reshaping healthcare delivery and diagnostics, though experts emphasize that building trust requires investment in the \"human stack\" alongside technology, and regulators globally are grappling with how to oversee generative AI tools in clinical settings.",
